    "case_id": "182",
    "case_summary": "A 62-year-old woman with IgG kappa plasma cell myeloma (Durie-Salmon IIA, R-ISS II), previously treated with multiple chemotherapy regimens, two autologous stem-cell transplants, thalidomide maintenance, and focal bone radiotherapy, underwent routine surveillance imaging.  \nShe was asymptomatic with stable vital signs and no hepatosplenomegaly on examination. Liver function tests, including ALT, AST, ALP, GGTP, and bilirubin, were within normal limits.  \nOn PET/CT, a 35 mm lesion in the right hepatic lobe showed an SUVmax of 6.1. Contrast-enhanced MRI of the abdomen demonstrated a 17 mm lesion in segment VI with moderately increased T2 signal, decreased T1 signal, weak heterogeneous contrast enhancement, and restricted diffusion, favoring a non-characteristic malignant lesion.  \nSerial imaging over subsequent months showed a decrease in lesion size to 10 mm, followed by enlargement to 57 × 46 mm.  \nGiven these findings, a percutaneous liver biopsy was planned to establish a definitive diagnosis.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Plasmacytoma\nB. Hepatocellular carcinoma considered\nC. Hepatic adenoma considered\nD. Hypervascular metastases considered (melanoma, neuroendocrine tumors, renal cell carcinoma, thyroid carcinoma)\nE. Extramedullary plasmacytoma considered given her history of multiple myeloma\nF. None\nG. None\nH. None\n",
